 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Kanem-Bornu grew around Lake Chad, including part of today's northern Cameroon.\nKanem, east of Lake Chad, was founded by about the 9th century and ruled by the Saifawa dynasty. Its rulers took the title Mai. Mai Hummay became a Muslim at the end of the 11th century. The capital was Njimi.\nIn the late 14th century the Bulala attacked Kanem, and the Mai moved west of the lake to Bornu. Ali Gaji built a new capital, Ngazargamu, in the 15th century.\nMai Idris Alooma (ruled about 1571–1603) was its greatest ruler: he bought firearms (muskets) and hired Turkish musketeers, used camels and boats, reformed justice by Islamic law and built mosques.\nWealth: trans-Saharan trade with Tripoli and Egypt (slaves, ivory, leather traded for horses, salt, cloth and weapons), and taxes.\nDecline: weak rulers, attacks by the Fulani jihad (19th century) and by Rabih (1893).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
